(I just submitted this bug message to the NetHack development team. It
occurs with the official OS X tiles port of NetHack 3.4.3 running on Mac
OS X 10.3.5. I am not able to test for this on other platforms or with
other game versions.)

The bug is specific to wizard mode.

When a wizard kit file contains a character's quest artifact, the game
aborts. When the same character wishes for the quest artifact during
game play, or when the artifact appears in the wizard kit but is not
that character's quest artifact, the game runs properly.

Steps to reproduce:

1) Create a wizard kit file that contains a quest artifact (e.g., the
Orb of Fate).
2) Set the exported environment variable WIZKIT to reference that file.
3) Run a new NetHack game in wizard mode.
4) Elect to play the character role for whom that artifact is the quest
artifact (valkyrie in this case).
5) Once you complete the new character profile, press <Play>.
6) The game window partially paints, then the game aborts.

If you run NetHack from Terminal, the game emits "Bus error" to stderr
before aborting. If your have level checkpoints turned on, you'll find a
5##wizard.0 checkpoint file left in the playground after program
termination.

If you instead create a character for whom the artifact is not the quest
artifact -- anything but valkyrie in this example -- the wiz-kit works
fine, and the game runs normally. So the problem is not having a quest
artifact in the wizard kit per-se. Rather, the problem is having a
particular character role's quest artifact in that role's wizard kit.

Suggested fix:

Change the code to allow a character's quest artifact to appear in the
character's wizard kit. I'm guessing -- but haven't looked at the code
to confirm -- that the bug relates to the dialog that normally pops up
in wizard mode when you wish for your own quest artifact. Since the
wizard kit file is read before any game dialogs can be answered, perhaps
the code is getting into a confused state.

If this fix is too complex or untestable, or if you believe a character
should not be allowed to have her quest artifact in her wizard kit, then
have the game emit a diagnostic message when parsing the wizard kit file
(much as happens now when the game parses an ill-formed line in the
wizard kit file).

